<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <application
        android:label="Salapify 3"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"><!--
        Data stays on the device, full stop. Ported unchanged from the shipped
        app, where it is ADR 0002. It is written out again here rather than
        assumed, because `flutter create` leaves allowBackup UNSET, which
        Android reads as true: the scaffolded app would have been eligible for
        Google auto backup on its first build, and test/core/data/
        backup_posture_test.dart caught exactly that on its first run.

        Android Auto Backup is OFF because the app holds the whole ledger
        locally. With backup on, that data would be uploaded to the user's
        Google Drive, which contradicts both the encrypted-at-rest promise and
        the published privacy policy's "stays on your phone". It would also
        break a restore onto a new phone, because the SQLCipher key lives in
        the Android Keystore and is NOT backed up, so a restored encrypted
        database could not be opened.

        allowBackup=false is the primary control and not the whole story. On
        Android 12 and newer it disables cloud backup but does NOT stop a
        device-to-device transfer, which is governed by the <device-transfer>
        section of data_extraction_rules.xml. So:
          - fullBackupContent -> res/xml/backup_rules.xml (Android 11 and lower)
          - dataExtractionRules -> res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml (12+)
        Both exclude every data domain, so financial data leaves this phone
        only by the user's own backup export, on every Android version.
        Removing either attribute, or flipping allowBackup, reddens the posture
        test. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
